            <div xml:id="d623">

                <pb facs="0583" n="575" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg575"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (Requisitions)/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-06T18:00:00-04:00">June 6, 1940—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>183. Your 418, June 6, 11 a.m.<anchor n="94" xml:id="d623-f1"/> The Department
                    has no objection to your communicating orally to Japanese officials and to the
                    press, as an <pb facs="0584" n="576" xml:id="pg576"/> informal expression of
                    your understanding of this phase of the Government’s national defense program,
                    the substance of the Department’s 179, June 4, 1 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >10</hi>] p.m., but it is not desired that the language of that telegram be
                    given textually to the press or published in the press as a quotation.</p>
                <p>Some of the statements contained in the telegram in question have been orally
                    communicated in informal language to press correspondents here, but no statement
                    in the matter is being issued by the Department at the present time.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
